bove $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551271 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45127938-c6a2-5a13-8b44-5358b2433862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551271 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{33}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{41}$ equal to zero.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "634c0345-e97a-5738-8ae3-640a8e78ce93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551282 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{41}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adab6899-9f13-599d-9b9d-313f4a53055c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551323 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's make entries above ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
